Ladybird displays HTML and SVG embeds in a child navigable, and raster
embeds as images. Blink’s HTMLEmbedElement::UpdatePluginInternal()
and WebKit’s HTMLEmbedElement::updateWidget() delegate loading
to their plugin and frame loaders. Gecko’s HTMLEmbedElement uses
nsObjectLoadingContent::LoadObject() to select a representation.

Chrome 158.0.8127.36, Firefox 151.0 and WebKit 26.5 all gave HTML a
child window and a 300 by 150 viewport. SVG had a child window and used
its natural size. PNG had its natural size and no child window. Chrome
and WebKit suppressed a display:none embed. Firefox loaded its document.

Ladybird applies the embedding document’s resource policy before
decoding a raster response. A denied response clears the previous
representation. With the document’s embedder policy set to require-corp,
Chrome, Firefox and WebKit all displayed the 100 by 50 image from the
same origin and from a second origin that explicitly allowed embedding.

An embed in an object’s fallback content starts loading when the
object starts showing that content, and its load delays the document’s
load event. In Chrome 158, Firefox 151, and WebKit 26.5, such an embed
fired its load event before the window’s. Firefox loaded it again each
time the object returned to its fallback content, as Ladybird does.
Chrome kept the embed loaded while the object showed an image.

When window.stop() stops an embed’s fetch before its response, Ladybird
stops delaying the load event on it, so the document’s readyState
reaches “complete”. For an image, Chrome 158 and Firefox 151 report
“complete” right after window.stop() too, and Chrome fires an error
event at the embed; WebKit 26.5 keeps “interactive”. None of the three
fires a load event at the window then.

Ladybird loads an embed in a shadow tree once it’s connected, though the
spec’s “in a document” condition hasn’t been updated for shadow trees.
Chrome 158, Firefox 151, and WebKit 26.5 all loaded an image embed and a
document embed at the top of an open shadow root, at 100 by 50 and 300
by 150. And when window.stop() stops an embed’s fetch while the body of
a raster image is still arriving, Ladybird ends the image too, so that
the document’s readyState reaches “complete”.

Removing an embed while the body of its raster image is still arriving
ends that image, so it no longer delays the load event. Firefox 151
fired the window’s load event right after the removal; Chrome 158
finished loading the image, and fired it then.
